/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Each stage takes one description of the chip, produces a more concrete one and hands a checked file to the next.
   - Some syllabi call this the ASIC design flow.
   - After tapeout the foundry makes one photomask per layer and builds hundreds of identical dies on a silicon wafer, today usually 300 mm across.
   - Yield = good dies ÷ total dies.
   - At block level the flow fixes the details that sink projects late: the clock plan (PLL, clock domains, resets), the I/O ring (pads, electrostatic discharge protection), the device modes (functional, scan, self-test, sleep), the memories (co
   - A complex chip or SoC is hard to verify and test because: (1) its state space is too large to simulate fully, so teams measure coverage and add formal proofs and emulation; (2) embedded IP cores sit deep inside, so their pins are not chip p
   - The design team hands the test team three things: the DUT (device under test, as a netlist), the inserted DFT hardware, and the pattern set.
   - In scan design each flip-flop gets a multiplexer, and in test mode the flip-flops join into a shift register (a scan chain): a pattern is shifted in, captured in one clock and shifted out.
   - N is the number of patterns, L the longest chain in flip-flops, Tclk the scan clock period and the tester rate in rupees per second.
*/
