/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A standard part is designed once and sold to many customers: a 74-series gate, a DRAM, a general microcontroller.
   - ASICs are classified by what is made only for this design.
   - Some syllabi group standard-cell and gate-array ASICs together as semicustom ASICs.
   - In full-custom design the engineer sizes and draws every transistor in a layout editor, then iterates DRC, LVS and extraction by hand.
   - In standard-cell design the gates come from a library: inverters, NAND, NOR, AND-OR-invert, multiplexers, flip-flops and adder cells, each in several drive strengths.
   - Synthesis picks cells, placement puts them in rows and routing joins them in metal above the cells.
   - A masked gate array (MGA) starts from a base wafer that the foundry has already processed up to the transistors.
   - Not every transistor of a base can be used: routing blocks some of them, so a design can fill only part of the gates, and the usable fraction depends on the array and the design.
   - N is the number of chips and c the cost of each chip.
   - Cost is not the only test.
*/
